## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates a practical monocular solution for tool manipulation that must handle new categories, operate under strict latency constraints, and support few-shot on-device adaptation in cluttered workshop environments.

## Key Concepts
- Monocular 6-DoF pose estimation from 2D-3D correspondences
- Few-shot on-device adaptation using adapter modules
- Synthetic and real data strategy with domain randomization
- Real-time performance requirements and edge GPU optimizations

## Code Example
```javascript
// PnP pose estimation sketch
function estimatePose2D3D(keypoints2D, model3D, camera) {
  // use perspective-n-point algorithm
  return cv.solvePnP(model3D, keypoints2D, camera.matrix, camera.distortion);
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests the ability to combine learned features with classical cues under tight on-device latency, in a realistic retail robotics setting.

## Key Concepts
- Lightweight CNN + classical feature fusion
- On-device few-shot adaptation (3-5 shots)
- Latency budgeting (<=50 ms/frame)
- Robust evaluation: per-item accuracy, misorientation metrics, throughput

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-code sketch of forward pass
function forward(frame) {
  const rois = detectROIs(frame);
  for (const roi of rois) {
    const cnnFeat = smallCNN(roi);
    const cues = extractCues(roi); // hist, edges, texture
    const fused = fuse(cnnFeat, cues);
    const label = orientationHead(fused);
    if (label === 'misoriented') alert(roi);
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle label drift with new item designs?
- What ablation studies would you run to verify cue contributions?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests a practical, beginner-friendly CV pipeline for real-time counting under occlusion and lighting changes. Emphasizes fallbacks to simple cues and a tiny classifier, plus lightweight tracking for stability.

## Key Concepts
- ROI extraction and scene normalization
- Blob-based counting from simple cues
- Lightweight classifier for type refinement
- Frame-to-frame tracking (Kalman)

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudocode: basic blob counting and simple classifier usage
function countItems(frameROI, types) {
  const blobs = segmentByColorAndEdges(frameROI);
  const counts = {};
  for (const t of types) counts[t] = 0;
  for (const b of blobs) {
    const type = classifyBlob(b, t); // tiny CNN or heuristic
    if (type) counts[type] += 1;
  }
  return counts;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle new product types without retraining the classifier?
- What are failure modes with reflective packaging or transparent wrappers?
